Actually I think the spec is saying there are three valid forms:
1. `new_grep PATTERN` - no files, search stdin
2. `new_grep PATTERN FILE...` - one or more files
3. `new_grep -- PATTERN FILE...` - same as 2 but with `--` before pattern

So when we have `./build/new_grep -- hello /tmp/test`:
- Pattern is "hello"
- File is "/tmp/test"

And when we have `./build/new_grep hello /tmp/test`:
- Pattern is "hello"  
- File is "/tmp/test"

The difference is just that in form 3, `--` appears before the pattern to indicate "stop treating args as options".

So my logic should be:
1. Check for unknown options starting from index 1
2. If we see `--`, mark it and continue checking (but don't treat subsequent args as options)
3. Pattern is always argv[1] UNLESS argv[1] == "--", in which case pattern is argv[2]
4. Files start at argv[2] if no `--`, or argv[3] if there's a `--`

